WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
Here's a habit NESA's marking feedback keeps flagging.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:05.920
In eight of the last twelve Chemistry and

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.120
Biology reports, it points out comparisons written as

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:13.200
two separate descriptions: everything about one thing, then

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:14.560
everything about the other.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.280
Each half can be right, and the answer

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:18.560
still doesn't compare.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.400
In recent Biology papers, compare questions were worth

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:23.840
three to six marks.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.800
So let's learn to write in pairs.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.840
Here's what NESA's glossary says, in short.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:34.480
Compare means show similarities or differences.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:38.400
Contrast means show differences, or opposites.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.760
Distinguish means show how things differ.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.360
So contrast and distinguish want differences.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:48.400
Compare can take both, and the safe habit

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.560
is to give at least one similarity and

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.040
one difference, unless the question narrows it.

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:55.600
In the last five years of Chemistry and

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.360
Biology written questions, compare appeared seven times.

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:02.960
Contrast and distinguish didn't, but they're still glossary

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.320
words, so know them.

00:01:05.534 --> 00:01:08.413
Here's an HSC-style Biology question.

00:01:08.734 --> 00:01:12.093
Red snapdragons were crossed with white snapdragons, and

00:01:12.174 --> 00:01:14.053
every offspring was pink.

00:01:14.414 --> 00:01:16.973
Red cattle were crossed with white cattle, and

00:01:17.054 --> 00:01:19.693
every calf was roan: a coat with red

00:01:19.774 --> 00:01:21.773
hairs and white hairs mixed together.

00:01:22.414 --> 00:01:24.493
All the parents were true-breeding.

00:01:24.814 --> 00:01:27.213
The question asks you to compare how colour

00:01:27.294 --> 00:01:29.213
is inherited in the two crosses.

00:01:29.694 --> 00:01:30.693
Three marks.

00:01:30.974 --> 00:01:33.613
If you take Chemistry, watch the shape: there's

00:01:33.694 --> 00:01:35.373
a Chemistry one coming for you.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.120
Run the routine first.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:41.000
The verb is compare, so you need what's

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.400
the same and what differs, about the same

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:44.040
feature.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.920
Three marks suggests about three paired points.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:49.480
Underline the givens.

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:53.080
True-breeding tells you each parent has two identical

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:53.800
alleles.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:56.840
The parents differ, so every offspring gets one

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.800
of each: heterozygous.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:01.480
Pink, and red and white hairs, are the

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.800
two results you'll set side by side.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.920
There's no constraint phrase, so any correct inheritance

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:08.040
reasoning counts.

00:02:09.267 --> 00:02:10.146
Here's the trick.

00:02:10.547 --> 00:02:13.906
Before you write, sketch a tiny table: one

00:02:13.987 --> 00:02:16.866
row per feature, one column for each case.

00:02:17.427 --> 00:02:21.186
Row one, the offspring's genotype: both crosses give

00:02:21.267 --> 00:02:22.386
heterozygotes.

00:02:22.867 --> 00:02:26.386
Row two, the offspring against their parents: in

00:02:26.467 --> 00:02:29.346
both, they look different from both parents.

00:02:29.667 --> 00:02:32.386
Row three, how the two alleles show.

00:02:32.787 --> 00:02:35.346
In snapdragons, the colour is in between.

00:02:35.747 --> 00:02:38.706
In cattle, both colours show, hair by hair.

00:02:39.267 --> 00:02:42.146
Now every row lines up one feature across

00:02:42.307 --> 00:02:43.346
both cases.

00:02:44.567 --> 00:02:47.366
Now turn each row into one sentence, with

00:02:47.447 --> 00:02:48.806
a comparison word.

00:02:49.127 --> 00:02:52.406
Row one: in both crosses, the offspring are

00:02:52.487 --> 00:02:56.326
heterozygous, because each true-breeding parent gives a different

00:02:56.407 --> 00:02:57.446
allele.

00:02:57.567 --> 00:03:01.766
Row two: both heterozygotes look unlike either parent,

00:03:01.847 --> 00:03:04.806
so neither allele completely masks the other.

00:03:05.127 --> 00:03:09.766
Row three: snapdragons show incomplete dominance, an in-between

00:03:09.847 --> 00:03:13.846
pink, whereas cattle show co-dominance, with both colours

00:03:13.967 --> 00:03:16.486
fully expressed in separate hairs.

00:03:16.647 --> 00:03:20.166
Three paired sentences: in our marking guide, three

00:03:20.247 --> 00:03:20.966
marks.

00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:24.646
These little words do the comparing for you.

00:03:25.127 --> 00:03:29.206
For similarities: both, in both, similarly.

00:03:29.447 --> 00:03:34.006
For differences: whereas, while, but, unlike,

00:03:34.087 --> 00:03:37.206
and words ending in er, like larger or

00:03:37.367 --> 00:03:38.086
slower.

00:03:38.407 --> 00:03:41.126
But a comparison word can't rescue a mismatched

00:03:41.207 --> 00:03:41.806
pair.

00:03:42.087 --> 00:03:45.526
"Snapdragons are pink, whereas cattle have hair" uses

00:03:45.607 --> 00:03:48.966
whereas, but it compares colour with hair.

00:03:49.047 --> 00:03:52.086
Same feature, both sides, one sentence.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:54.900
Now here's a weaker answer.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:58.980
"Snapdragons show incomplete dominance, and their offspring are

00:03:59.180 --> 00:03:59.460
pink.

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:03.140
Cattle show co-dominance, and the calves are roan,

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:04.580
with red and white hairs."

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:06.540
Every word is right.

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:08.180
But it's two descriptions.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:11.060
There's no similarity at all, and the difference

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:12.660
is left for the marker to find.

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:15.060
In our marking guide it earns one of

00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:17.860
three, for the correct pattern in each case.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.180
The fix isn't more science.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:21.460
It's pairing.

00:04:22.667 --> 00:04:25.586
NESA's marking guidelines point the same way.

00:04:25.867 --> 00:04:28.906
In one recent three-mark compare question, the sample

00:04:28.987 --> 00:04:32.266
answer was only two sentences: a similarity, and

00:04:32.347 --> 00:04:34.906
a difference, each about the same feature of

00:04:35.067 --> 00:04:35.866
both things.

00:04:36.427 --> 00:04:39.066
In a table question, comparing all the features

00:04:39.227 --> 00:04:42.026
correctly reached three marks, and comparing most of

00:04:42.107 --> 00:04:43.226
them reached two.

00:04:43.707 --> 00:04:45.946
So the marks followed complete pairing of the

00:04:46.027 --> 00:04:48.826
features asked about, not longer descriptions.

00:04:49.307 --> 00:04:52.266
The criteria are set question by question, but

00:04:52.347 --> 00:04:54.666
pairing turns up again and again.

00:04:55.867 --> 00:04:56.426
Your turn.

00:04:56.507 --> 00:04:59.226
Do the one in your subject, or both.

00:04:59.467 --> 00:05:00.426
Chemistry first.

00:05:01.227 --> 00:05:03.946
Two solutions are both zero point one zero

00:05:04.107 --> 00:05:08.306
mole per litre: hydrochloric acid and ethanoic acid.

00:05:08.667 --> 00:05:11.626
The table shows their conductivity, how they react

00:05:11.707 --> 00:05:14.426
with magnesium ribbon, and the volume of sodium

00:05:14.627 --> 00:05:17.266
hydroxide each needs to be neutralised.

00:05:17.627 --> 00:05:20.346
Using the data, compare the two solutions in

00:05:20.427 --> 00:05:21.866
terms of acid strength.

00:05:22.427 --> 00:05:23.146
Three marks.

00:05:23.627 --> 00:05:24.906
Pause here to answer.

00:05:35.334 --> 00:05:39.173
Two constraints: the data, and acid strength.

00:05:39.494 --> 00:05:44.053
Similarity: both need twenty millilitres of sodium hydroxide:

00:05:44.134 --> 00:05:46.373
same moles of acid, and the weak acid

00:05:46.534 --> 00:05:48.693
keeps ionising as base is added.

00:05:49.334 --> 00:05:53.813
Difference: hydrochloric acid is strong and fully ionised,

00:05:53.934 --> 00:05:56.933
whereas ethanoic acid is weak, about one percent

00:05:57.014 --> 00:05:58.133
ionised.

00:05:58.254 --> 00:06:01.653
So hydrochloric acid has more ions: it conducts

00:06:01.814 --> 00:06:03.573
better and bubbles faster.

00:06:03.894 --> 00:06:06.613
The habit to drop: one acid, then the

00:06:06.694 --> 00:06:07.173
other.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:09.520
Now Biology.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.480
The diagram shows one pair of chromosomes in

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:15.200
a body cell, after DNA replication.

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:19.840
Distinguish between homologous chromosomes and sister chromatids.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:21.040
Two marks.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:22.800
Pause here to answer.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:38.320
Distinguish means differences: two marks, two paired differences.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:43.040
Origin: homologous chromosomes come one from each parent,

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:45.760
whereas sister chromatids are two copies of one

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:48.560
chromosome, made by DNA replication.

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:53.040
Alleles: homologous chromosomes carry the same genes but

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:56.480
may carry different alleles, whereas sister chromatids are

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:57.280
identical.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:01.200
The habit to drop: two quick definitions, with

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:02.800
no feature they differ in.

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:08.840
So: compare, give similarities and differences, in pairs.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:13.280
Contrast and distinguish, differences only, still in pairs.

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:16.640
Sketch the table, one row per feature, then

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:19.440
write one sentence per row, with both or

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:20.720
whereas.

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:25.760
Next: the judgement verbs, analyse, assess, evaluate and

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.440
justify, where you make a call and back

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:28.880
it up.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:31.540
Here are the key notes.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:33.700
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:34.580
to copy them.
